              21:00  TOTAL time spent

I discussed my solution with no one. I used the Go documentation for help
understanding Go syntax and standard library features.

The biggest difficulty I encountered was getting comfortable with Go's
concurrency model and unfamiliar syntax. In particular, it took some time to
understand the distinction between send-only and receive-only channels and how
goroutines communicate through channels. I also initially found the difference
between a Fetcher and a channel confusing, since both can be involved in
producing values, but they serve different purposes. Another major conceptual
challenge was synchronization. Understanding when to use a mutex versus a
semaphore, and how semaphore permits are acquired and released, required some
careful thought. The custom Semaphore implementation was especially difficult
because I had to reason about what the semaphore value represented and why
Post() either increments the available permits or directly wakes a waiting
goroutine. I also spent time becoming comfortable with Go-specific features
such as generics, zero values, context cancellation, atomic values, and the use
of defer for synchronization cleanup. The Go documentation was particularly
helpful for resolving syntax questions and understanding these standard library
features.
